(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-cms-exam-6-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 6 Master Exam — Form C","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 22-year-old woman had asthma as a young child, then had no symptoms throughout adolescence. Over the past 2 months her episodic wheezing and night cough have returned. Which statement best explains her course?","choices":["Her first diagnosis must have been wrong","Asthma can return in adulthood","Wheeze returning at 22 means cardiac asthma","Asthma that remits in adolescence stays gone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Asthma presents at any age, with a peak at 3 years; it may become asymptomatic during adolescence and may return in adulthood. Her childhood asthma, quiet teenage years and returning symptoms follow that pattern. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman with human immunodeficiency virus infection and chronic kidney disease is being treated with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for Pneumocystis pneumonia. Which tests should be monitored during treatment?","choices":["Lactate dehydrogenase trend","Blood count, creatinine, potassium","Beta-D-glucan trend","Weekly chest radiographs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. During Pneumocystis treatment the complete blood count, creatinine, potassium and drug toxicity are monitored, and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is adjusted for kidney function. Her chronic kidney disease makes the creatinine and potassium checks especially important. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man with minimal smoking history has emphysema with a post-bronchodilator ratio of 0.61. His chest x-ray shows the changes mainly in the lower lobes. Which test is most appropriate to find the cause?","choices":["Arterial blood gas","Alpha-1 antitrypsin level and genotype","Computed tomography of the chest","B-type natriuretic peptide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patients under 45, or without significant exposure, should be evaluated for alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, and basilar emphysema adds to the suspicion. A level below 11 micromol/L with a severely deficient genotype is diagnostic. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 10, 19, 30"},{"q":"Which cause of acute respiratory acidosis is a central nervous system depressant?","choices":["Thyroxine excess","Intravenous sedation","Caffeine excess","Salicylate toxicity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Central nervous system depression from recreational drugs, an intracranial catastrophe or intravenous sedation causes acute respiratory acidosis by suppressing breathing, so carbon dioxide is retained. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which physical findings may accompany extrinsic (allergic) asthma?","choices":["Clubbing, crackles, and weight loss","Butterfly rash, arthritis, and fever","Swollen nasal mucosa, eczema, and nasal polyps","Cyanosis, edema, and distended neck veins"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Extrinsic, allergic asthma may bring swelling of the nasal mucosa, increased secretions, atopic dermatitis or eczema, and nasal polyps, even when the chest exam is clear between attacks. These reflect the atopic triad of rhinitis, asthma and dermatitis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man who aspirated gastric contents during a witnessed seizure was managed with oxygen and airway support and no antibiotic. Three days later he is worse, with a new fever, purulent sputum and a persistent infiltrate. Which complication should be suspected?","choices":["Viral acute bronchitis","Asthma exacerbation","Resolving chemical injury","Secondary bacterial infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pneumonitis is managed with support, and persistence or worsening raises concern for secondary infection, a complication or another diagnosis. New fever, purulent sputum and a persistent infiltrate after 3 days point to a secondary bacterial infection, which now needs the applicable pneumonia regimen. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man in septic shock has a lactic acidosis. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.27 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 25 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 11 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). How does his acidemia affect oxygen release from hemoglobin to his tissues?","choices":["Release increases; the curve shifts right","Release decreases; the curve shifts left","Release is unchanged by pH","Release decreases; the curve shifts right"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acidosis decreases hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen and shifts the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the right, so hemoglobin releases oxygen more readily and delivery to the tissues increases. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which form of tobacco exposure has evidence as an asthma risk factor?","choices":["Maternal smoking","Smoking by a relative who lives elsewhere","Nicotine replacement patches","Smoking that began after age 60"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Under passive cigarette smoke there is evidence that maternal smoking is a risk factor for asthma, although its effect is difficult to separate from the increased risk of respiratory infections in exposed children. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"An 80-year-old woman with advanced chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, frequent admissions and declining function lives with her daughter, who helps with her care. She has not discussed her wishes for future care. Which step is most appropriate now?","choices":["Wait for the final days of life","Integrate palliative care early","Add triple therapy and recheck in a year","Focus on her returning to work"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. For older adults, palliative care is integrated early, with caregiver involvement and advance directives, and priorities shift to function, quality of life, multimorbidity and goals of care. Her daughter's involvement makes it timely. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with community-acquired pneumonia is newly disoriented to place and time. His blood urea nitrogen is 31 mg/dL, respiratory rate 32/min and blood pressure 86/52 mm Hg. What is his CURB-65 (confusion, urea, respiratory rate, blood pressure, age 65 or older) score?","choices":["2","3","4","5"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Confusion 1 (new disorientation) + urea 1 (31 is above 20) + respiratory rate 1 (32 is 30 or more) + blood pressure 1 (systolic below 90) + age 0 (58) = 4. That is high severity, with a risk of death of 15 to 40%: hospital, urgent senior review and a decision about critical care, especially at 4 or 5. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 29, 31"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with chronic kidney failure who has missed several dialysis sessions is nauseated and fatigued. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.26 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 29 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 13 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 138 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 100 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis","Respiratory acidosis","Elevated anion gap metabolic acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.26 is acidemia, and the bicarbonate of 13 is low and matches it: metabolic. Gap = 138 - (100 + 13) = 25, above 9 to 15, so it is elevated; uremia is an elevated-gap cause. The carbon dioxide of 29 is compensation (Winter 1.5 x 13 + 8 = 27.5, plus or minus 2). — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with septic shock and lactic acidosis is tiring. His bicarbonate is 12 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L) and his carbon dioxide is 38 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg). By the Winter formula, what does his carbon dioxide show?","choices":["An added respiratory alkalosis","Appropriate compensation","Normal, since 38 is in range","An added respiratory acidosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Winter formula: 1.5 x 12 + 8 = 26, plus or minus 2, so 24 to 28. His carbon dioxide of 38 is well above it: metabolic acidosis together with respiratory acidosis, which can signal impending respiratory failure in a tiring patient. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman has a post-bronchodilator ratio of 0.66 and a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 83% predicted. Which airflow grade fits?","choices":["Grade 1 (mild)","Grade 2 (moderate)","Grade 3 (severe)","Grade 4 (very severe)"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her ratio of 0.66 confirms obstruction, and a post-bronchodilator 1-second volume of 83% predicted meets the grade 1 (mild) threshold of 80% or more. Her airflow is near normal even though the ratio is abnormal. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman who keeps several parakeets at home is diagnosed with psittacosis and started on doxycycline. One of the birds has been sick. Which additional step is appropriate?","choices":["Respiratory isolation at home","Reporting is not required","Veterinary assessment of the birds","Prophylaxis for household contacts"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Management includes arranging veterinary assessment of implicated birds and safe cleaning of contaminated areas, reporting according to state requirements and involving public health for outbreaks. Her sick parakeet is the likely source and can infect others. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man with community-acquired pneumonia confirmed on chest radiograph is stable for outpatient treatment. He has no comorbidities, no severe features and no resistant-organism risk. Which test is low value and should be skipped?","choices":["Vital signs and sepsis screen","Pulse oximetry","Severity index scoring","Routine sputum culture"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cultures are obtained selectively, for severe community-acquired pneumonia, resistant-pathogen risk or treatment failure, and routine cultures do not help every stable outpatient. He has none of those triggers, so a culture would not change any decision. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 29, 125"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease who smokes is asked about his smoking and is clearly advised to quit, with a link to his diabetes and heart risk. Using the Five A's approach, which step should come next?","choices":["Assess his readiness to change","Ask about his smoking again","Assist with a quit plan","Arrange a follow-up visit"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. After Ask and Advise, the Five A's continue with Assess, evaluating the person's readiness and willingness to change, then Assist with actionable steps, and Arrange follow-up. Readiness shapes how the clinician helps. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has an exacerbation with marked breathlessness. Before attributing it to the exacerbation, his clinician wants to exclude acute heart failure. Which test is most useful for that purpose?","choices":["B-type natriuretic peptide","6-minute walk test","Alpha-1 antitrypsin level","Spirometry with a bronchodilator"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Exacerbation evaluation includes oxygen saturation, a blood gas, a chest x-ray, an electrocardiogram, and B-type natriuretic peptide or troponin. B-type natriuretic peptide is the test for suspected associated heart failure, one of the diagnoses to exclude. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 30, 56"},{"q":"Which statement about sputum production in this disease is accurate?","choices":["It is present in every patient","Its absence rules out the disease","It reflects chronic bronchitis","It is typical of pure emphysema"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Patients do not always produce sputum: it is seen with chronic bronchitis or a combined presentation and is typically not prevalent in emphysema-only cases. Its absence does not exclude the disease. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man with extrinsic asthma flares whenever he cleans his apartment, and allergy skin testing shows sensitivity to house dust mites. His controller and rescue inhalers are prescribed. Which non-drug measure should be added?","choices":["Start allergen immunotherapy at home","Limit his physical activity","Reduce dust mite exposure at home","Move from the city to a rural area"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Non-pharmacological asthma treatment is trigger avoidance and environmental control. Dust mites are the most common inhaled allergen trigger, and his skin test and cleaning-related flares identify it, so reducing his exposure at home is the added measure. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 38, 67"},{"q":"What is the most common pathogen in community-acquired pneumonia?","choices":["Haemophilus influenzae","Mycoplasma pneumoniae","Klebsiella pneumoniae","Streptococcus pneumoniae"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Streptococcus pneumoniae (pneumococcus) is the most common pathogen in community-acquired pneumonia. It is the usual cause of lobar pneumonia on the radiograph, and empiric therapy for community-acquired pneumonia covers it. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 24, 23"},{"q":"Besides delivering medication, which airway problem is a rationale for nebulizer therapy?","choices":["Reducing carbon dioxide retention","Correcting a fixed airway narrowing","Secretion clearance","Replacing an endotracheal tube"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Secretion clearance is a listed rationale for nebulizer therapy, in addition to delivering liquid medication directly to the airways. The other rationales are choice of medication, inhaler difficulty and severe exacerbations. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman with moderate persistent asthma was given salmeterol by itself at an urgent care visit and it has been her sole controller for a month. Which change does her regimen need?","choices":["Add an inhaled corticosteroid","Replace it with tiotropium","Continue salmeterol alone","Switch it to cromolyn sodium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A long-acting beta-2 agonist such as salmeterol has no anti-inflammatory effect and should not be used as monotherapy; it is used in combination with an inhaled corticosteroid. Adding one treats her airway inflammation and may allow control at a lower steroid dose. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man has coronavirus disease 2019 with fever and cough. His oxygen saturation is 91% on room air, his respiratory rate is 32/min, and imaging shows infiltrates over more than half of his lung volume. He is not in shock and does not need mechanical ventilation. Which severity category fits?","choices":["Mild illness","Moderate illness","Severe illness","Critical illness"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Severe illness is an oxygen saturation below 94% on room air, marked tachypnea or infiltrates over more than 50% of the lung volume, and he has all three. Critical illness needs acute respiratory failure, septic shock or multiple organ dysfunction, which he does not have. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and an echocardiogram showing pulmonary hypertension has a resting room-air saturation of 89%. Which statement about home oxygen applies to her?","choices":["She qualifies in Group I","She does not qualify at 89%","She qualifies for sleep oxygen alone","She qualifies in Group II"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her resting saturation of 89% is above the Group I cutoff of 88% or less, so on its own it would not qualify. The echocardiogram showing pulmonary hypertension adds the comorbidity that makes a saturation of 89% qualify, which is Group II (recertified at 3 months). — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 43-year-old woman with suspected pulmonary tuberculosis has three sputum specimens collected. Which testing should be requested on the specimens?","choices":["Gram stain and routine bacterial culture","Molecular test in place of culture","Acid-fast smear, then stop if negative","Acid-fast smear, culture and a molecular test"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The specimens go for acid-fast smear and mycobacterial culture, a rapid molecular test is performed on at least one respiratory specimen, and molecular and culture-based drug-susceptibility testing is obtained. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man on home oxygen for chronic obstructive pulmonary disease arrives with burns to his lips and nose after lighting a cigarette while wearing his cannula. Which hazard does this show?","choices":["Vasoconstriction","Mucosal irritation","Hypercapnia","Fire"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A man on home oxygen lights a cigarette while wearing his cannula and burns his lips and nose. Oxygen is highly flammable, and fire is a listed hazard of oxygen therapy, which is why no smoking is taught for every home oxygen user. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 7, 9"},{"q":"Which new symptom should prompt a patient with acute bronchitis to return for reassessment?","choices":["Hemoptysis","Green or yellow sputum","Mild chest tightness","Cough still present at 2 weeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Return precautions for acute bronchitis are dyspnea, hemoptysis, persistent fever, hypoxemia, or worsening after initial improvement. Expected-course counseling and these explicit precautions are the safety net when antibiotics are withheld. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 19, 126"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with no medical conditions has never received a pneumococcal vaccine. Which pneumococcal vaccination is appropriate for him?","choices":["One 20-valent conjugate vaccine dose","A 15-valent conjugate vaccine alone","Wait until he turns 65","Yearly pneumococcal vaccination"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All adults aged 50 or older who have never had a pneumococcal conjugate vaccine, or whose history is unknown, are vaccinated. One dose of the 20-valent or 21-valent conjugate vaccine completes the series; a 15-valent conjugate vaccine is usually followed by the 23-valent polysaccharide vaccine. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 8, 9, 124"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man arrived at a high-altitude camp two hours ago and is hyperventilating; his carbon dioxide is 25 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg). Using an acute bicarbonate fall of about 2 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg fall in carbon dioxide, from midpoints of 40 and 24, what bicarbonate is expected?","choices":["16.5 mEq/L","21 mEq/L","24 mEq/L","27 mEq/L"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Carbon dioxide fell 15 (40 to 25); acutely bicarbonate falls about 2 per 10, so 24 - 3 = 21 mEq/L. Two hours is acute; altitude is a listed hypoxic cause of hyperventilation. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman with long-standing asthma has had repeated exacerbations this year despite confirmed adherence and good inhaler technique. Her clinician suspects a hypersensitivity reaction to a mold. Which result supports that suspicion?","choices":["Normal antibody levels to Aspergillus","Raised immunoglobulin E and G to Aspergillus","A raised exhaled nitric oxide by itself","Negative skin tests with a normal immunoglobulin E"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis is a hypersensitivity reaction to Aspergillus fumigatus seen almost exclusively in asthma or cystic fibrosis. Exposure causes a vigorous immunoglobulin E and G response that leads to acute symptoms and exacerbations, as in this woman. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman lives in a high-risk congregate setting. She has no symptoms, no human immunodeficiency virus infection and no known recent tuberculosis contact. Her tuberculin skin test shows 11 mm of induration. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["Negative; her cut-off is 15 mm","Positive; disease is confirmed","Negative; she has no symptoms","Positive; her cut-off is 10 mm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. High-risk congregate settings are in the 10 mm group, with increased exposure risk, selected medical risks and children under 5. 11 mm meets 10 mm, so the result is positive; she then needs evaluation for disease before any preventive treatment. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 20, 22"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with confirmed chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a symptom assessment test score of 18 and a modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade of 2. She has had no exacerbations in the past year. Under the current three-group scheme, which group is she in?","choices":["Group A","Group E","Group B","Group D"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A symptom score of 18 (10 or more) and a dyspnea grade of 2 (2 or more) mean more symptoms, and no exacerbations means low risk. More symptoms with low risk is group B, which starts on a long-acting beta agonist plus a long-acting antimuscarinic. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 37, 40, 41"},{"q":"Which exposure is classic for histoplasmosis?","choices":["Farm animals or parturient cats","Hot tubs and fountains","Handling sick pet birds","Soil with bat or bird droppings"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Histoplasmosis is acquired from soil and bird or bat droppings, as in agricultural work, gardening and chicken coops. Geography, soil disruption and bird or bat exposure are asked about before testing. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 75, 77, 41"},{"q":"A 66-year-old thin man with a long smoking history breathes through pursed lips. He has a barrel chest, distant breath sounds and hyperresonance to percussion. His diffusing capacity is reduced, and he produces little sputum. Which subtype best describes his disease?","choices":["Pulmonary fibrosis","Chronic bronchitis subtype","Asthma","Emphysema subtype"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A thin patient with pursed-lip breathing, barrel chest, distant breath sounds and hyperresonance, little sputum, and a reduced diffusing capacity is the classic emphysema picture (the pink puffer), from alveolar destruction and loss of elastic recoil. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 15, 20"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man has latent tuberculosis infection; disease has been excluded, he takes no other medicines, and the source case's organism is fully susceptible. Which is a preferred short regimen?","choices":["Isoniazid daily for 9 months","Rifampin daily for 4 months","17-week rifapentine-moxifloxacin regimen","Four drugs for 2 months, then two for 4"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Daily rifampin for 4 months is one of the preferred short latent-infection regimens, along with weekly isoniazid plus rifapentine for 3 months and daily isoniazid plus rifampin for 3 months. Isoniazid alone for 6 or 9 months is the alternative when rifamycins are unsuitable. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 34, 37"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman with myasthenia gravis is hypoventilating, and her carbon dioxide is rising. Following water + carbon dioxide <-> carbonic acid <-> hydrogen ion + bicarbonate, what happens to her hydrogen ion concentration?","choices":["It rises once the kidneys respond","It is unchanged; bicarbonate absorbs it","It falls, raising her pH","It rises, lowering her pH"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Added carbon dioxide drives the carbonic acid reaction to the right, forming more carbonic acid and then more hydrogen ion and bicarbonate, so pH falls: hypoventilation, raised carbon dioxide, acidemia. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 36-year-old pet-shop employee has had a week of fever, severe headache, photophobia and a nonproductive cough. His pulse is 72/min with a temperature of 103°F, his spleen is palpable, and macular rashes on his face resemble the rose spots of typhoid fever. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Influenza","Psittacosis","Mycoplasma infection","Acute histoplasmosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Psittacosis is an occupational infection of pet-shop and zoo employees and poultry workers, caught from birds such as parakeets. Severe headache, photophobia, a dry cough, relative bradycardia, splenomegaly and Horder spots (facial macular rashes like typhoid rose spots) complete the picture. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 81, 82, 83, 75, 76, 89, 99"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has glaucoma treated with an oral carbonic anhydrase inhibitor, type 2 diabetes treated with metformin, and daily aspirin use for headaches. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.32 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 30 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 15 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 140 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 115 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Which of her medicines best accounts for this blood gas?","choices":["Metformin","A respiratory cause, not a medicine","Aspirin","The carbonic anhydrase inhibitor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Low pH and low bicarbonate make a metabolic acidosis; the gap is 140 - (115 + 15) = 10, within 9 to 15, with a high chloride: a normal-gap, hyperchloremic acidosis. Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors are on the normal-gap list (loss of bicarbonate). — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 47, 52, 55, 56, 58"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman develops ventilator-associated pneumonia on day 5 of mechanical ventilation. Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is common in her unit. Which empiric regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Cefepime as a single drug","Moxifloxacin plus linezolid","Cefepime plus vancomycin","Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Local methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus prevalence adds vancomycin or linezolid to an antipseudomonal beta-lactam such as cefepime. Cultures are obtained, and therapy is reassessed daily and de-escalated. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 46, 42, 48"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is taught to breathe out slowly through pursed lips when she is short of breath. She asks why it helps. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["It reduces mucus production","It increases inspiratory flow","It keeps her airways open","It slows her heart rate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pursed-lip breathing increases intra-airway pressure, which keeps the airways open during expiration and allows more gas exchange. It counters the collapse of airways that loss of elastic recoil causes in emphysema. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which statement about albuterol in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is accurate?","choices":["It is reserved for severe disease","It replaces long-acting therapy","Every patient should have one","Frequent use is harmless"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Every patient should have an albuterol inhaler to prevent or reduce symptoms, as needed. It should not be used excessively, because of toxicity and the risk of cardiac and psychiatric side effects. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a symptom assessment test score of 17 and a modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade of 2. She has had no exacerbations in the past year. Which initial maintenance regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Long-term azithromycin","Long-acting beta agonist plus antimuscarinic","Inhaled corticosteroid plus beta agonist","A single short-acting bronchodilator"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A score of 17 and a dyspnea grade of 2 mean more symptoms, and no exacerbations means low risk, which is group B. Group B starts on a long-acting beta agonist plus a long-acting antimuscarinic, ideally in a single inhaler for convenience. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 37, 40"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman is diagnosed with Mycobacterium avium complex lung disease. Her husband asks whether he needs testing because she is contagious, as with tuberculosis. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["He needs a tuberculin skin test now","He needs airborne precautions at home","Person-to-person spread is uncommon","She is contagious until treatment ends"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Most nontuberculous mycobacterial infections arise from environmental exposure, and person-to-person spread is uncommon. That is one reason species identification matters: transmission differs between nontuberculous mycobacteria and the tuberculosis complex. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 5, 38"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman who is 24 weeks pregnant has had 3 days of fever, myalgias and cough, and she is not short of breath. Influenza is strongly suspected, and a molecular test has been sent. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Start oseltamivir now","No antiviral after 48 hours","Await molecular confirmation","A single dose of baloxavir"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pregnancy in any trimester is a high-risk group, and oseltamivir is preferred for high-risk patients, including in pregnancy. It is started promptly, even after 48 hours of symptoms, without waiting for confirmation in a high-risk patient. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 98, 108, 95, 105"},{"q":"A 78-year-old woman received a respiratory syncytial virus vaccine last year and asks whether she needs another dose this fall. Which counseling statement is accurate?","choices":["Revaccinate every 10 years","No repeat dose is recommended","A second dose this fall","Revaccinate every year"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Respiratory syncytial virus vaccination is one dose for all adults aged 75 or older and for adults 50 to 74 at increased risk, and repeat annual dosing is not currently recommended. She already had her one dose. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 7, 124"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man with pneumonia is on a nasal cannula. His nurse turned the flow up to 8 L/min and his saturation is still 84%. He is alert and still ventilating well. Which is the next step?","choices":["Add a second nasal cannula","Turn the cannula up to 12 L/min","Change to a non-rebreather mask","Lower the flow to 2 L/min"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. His cannula is already above its limit: the cannula covers 1-6 L/min and its fraction tops out at 44%, so more turning gains nothing. He is still at 84% and still ventilating well, which is the listed use of the non-rebreather mask: severely hypoxic patients who are still ventilating well. At 10-15 L/min it delivers 60-100%, well above the cannula's ceiling. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 9, 11, 15"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman has new-onset persistent wheezing and cough that began last year. She has nasal polyps. Allergy skin tests are negative and her serum immunoglobulin E is normal. She had a wheezing episode after taking aspirin for a headache. Which type of asthma is most likely?","choices":["Intrinsic asthma","Extrinsic asthma","Catamenial asthma","Occupational asthma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Adult onset, persistent symptoms, nasal polyps, negative skin tests with a normal immunoglobulin E, and aspirin sensitivity all fit intrinsic asthma, the non-atopic form seen in about 10% of patients, which is usually more severe and persistent. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 34, 35"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with presumed infectious pulmonary tuberculosis is in an airborne infection isolation room. A nurse is about to enter her room. Which respiratory protection should the nurse wear?","choices":["A standard surgical mask","No mask if she is masked","Gloves and gown alone","Fit-tested N95 respirator"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Staff use fit-tested N95 or higher-level respiratory protection when caring for presumed or confirmed infectious tuberculosis. The organism spreads through aerosols released during coughing, speaking or singing, so the protection has to be respiratory. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has hypertension that is hard to control and muscle weakness. He takes no diuretics, has not vomited, and eats a bag of black licorice every day. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.47 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 47 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 33 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); potassium 3.0 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L); urine chloride is high. Which part of his history best accounts for this blood gas?","choices":["His daily black licorice","Anxiety with hyperventilation","Hidden vomiting","Antacid use"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. High pH with a high bicarbonate is metabolic alkalosis; the high urine chloride makes it saline unresponsive, and his high blood pressure puts it in the hypertensive group, which lists licorice. Low potassium with weakness fits. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 37-year-old woman with asthma records her peak flow at home. Over the past week her morning readings before albuterol have been about 280 L/min and her afternoon readings after albuterol about 380 L/min. What does this pattern indicate?","choices":["Good asthma control","Poor asthma control","Normal circadian variation needing no action","A faulty peak flow meter"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Peak flow is measured in the morning before and the afternoon after the bronchodilator, and a 20% change between them, or day to day, indicates poor control. A rise from 280 to 380 is about 36% of the morning value (26% of the afternoon value), well over the 20% line. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 22-year-old college athlete with gastroenteritis has vomited for three days and is dizzy on standing. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.51 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 48 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 37 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); potassium 3.1 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L); urine chloride is low. In addition to treating the cause, which therapy is indicated?","choices":["Starting a thiazide diuretic","A sodium bicarbonate infusion","Paper bag rebreathing","Isotonic saline"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Vomiting (loss of hydrochloric acid) with orthostatic dizziness and a low urine chloride is saline-responsive metabolic alkalosis, a sign of extracellular volume contraction. Isotonic saline corrects that volume deficit. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 35, 39, 42, 43, 44"},{"q":"At minimum, how often are adherence, symptoms and adverse effects reviewed during tuberculosis treatment?","choices":["Weekly","Monthly","Every 3 months","At the end of therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Adherence, symptoms and adverse effects are reviewed at least monthly, sputum cultures are followed until conversion, response is documented at 2 months, and liver injury, drug interactions and drug-specific toxicity are monitored. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman with asthma wakes most nights at about 3 in the morning with cough and wheeze, though she feels fine in the daytime. She asks why her symptoms come at night. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["Bronchial reactivity peaks in the early morning hours","Night symptoms point to vocal fold dysfunction","Her rescue inhaler wears off at night","Lying flat causes cardiac asthma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Asthma symptoms may be worse at night because of circadian variation in bronchomotor tone and bronchial reactivity, which peaks around 3 to 4 in the morning, the time she wakes. Night waking also counts when classifying severity. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman hospitalized with coronavirus disease 2019 and hypoxemia is on dexamethasone, but over 24 hours she needs rapidly increasing oxygen. Which treatment should be added?","choices":["Baricitinib or tocilizumab","Stop dexamethasone","Switch to molnupiravir","Add azithromycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rapid progression in a hospitalized patient may warrant baricitinib or tocilizumab in addition to corticosteroids, along with appropriate oxygen or ventilatory support. Her rising oxygen needs on dexamethasone are that rapid progression. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 121, 122"},{"q":"What does a chest radiograph usually show in asthma?","choices":["Pleural effusions","Bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy","Normal lungs or hyperinflation","Lobar consolidation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The chest radiograph in asthma is usually normal or may show hyperinflation from air trapping, so it supports rather than makes the diagnosis and mainly helps exclude other causes of symptoms. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man with mild persistent asthma well controlled on an inhaled corticosteroid dislikes inhalers and asks to switch to montelukast tablets alone. Which statement should guide the counseling?","choices":["It works as well as his inhaled steroid","It is less effective than his inhaled steroid","It is a long-acting beta-2 agonist","It is a rescue medicine for attacks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Montelukast is a leukotriene modifier with modest clinical benefit, useful as add-on therapy, and less effective than inhaled corticosteroids as monotherapy. His inhaled steroid is the preferred first-line treatment for persistent asthma. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 74, 83"},{"q":"An 84-year-old woman in hospice with metastatic cancer has air hunger (a distressing sense of not getting enough breath). Her saturation is 95%. Which therapy is a listed special indication?","choices":["Oxygen for symptom relief","An endotracheal tube","No oxygen, since saturation is normal","A home oxygen qualification test"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The hospice patient has air hunger, a distressing sense of not getting enough breath, and a saturation of 95%. Air hunger is a listed special indication, and symptom relief is a listed benefit of oxygen, so oxygen is given for comfort even without hypoxia. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 5, 21"},{"q":"What is the anion gap formula?","choices":["Sodium - (chloride + bicarbonate)","(Sodium + potassium) - chloride","1.5 x bicarbonate + 8","Chloride - (sodium + bicarbonate)"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The anion gap equals sodium minus the sum of chloride and bicarbonate, using serum electrolyte values. The normal anion gap is 9 to 15, and serum albumin accounts for most of it. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with chronic lung disease is drowsy and confused, with asterixis (a flapping tremor of the outstretched hands). Arterial blood gas: pH 7.22 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 75 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 30 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder best explains his findings?","choices":["Metabolic acidosis","Respiratory acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.22 is acidemia, and the carbon dioxide of 75 is high and matches it, so it is respiratory acidosis. Bicarbonate of 30 is the renal response. Somnolence, confusion and asterixis are its listed clinical findings, and chronic lung disease fits. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with alcoholism and chronic liver disease has community-acquired pneumonia and is stable for outpatient treatment. He had anaphylaxis to amoxicillin in the past. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Amoxicillin-clavulanate plus azithromycin","Doxycycline as a single drug","Amoxicillin as a single drug","Levofloxacin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Alcoholism and chronic liver disease are listed comorbidities, so he needs a beta-lactam plus azithromycin or doxycycline, or a respiratory fluoroquinolone (levofloxacin or moxifloxacin). His amoxicillin anaphylaxis removes the amoxicillin-based combination, leaving the fluoroquinolone, with a review of QT, interactions and fluoroquinolone risks. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 43"}]};
